Verilog-AMS defines random distribution functions, such as $rdist_normal.

== simulator level examples

sampling.gc:   random variable sampling and random constants
basic.gc:      run simulations with random parameters

== behavioural modelling

Can do the same as in "basic.gc" with a compiled model. See basic.vams.

$ gnucap-mg-vams --cc basic.vams | g++ -xc++ `gnucap-conf --cppflags` -fPIC -shared - -o basic.so
$ gnucap basic_compiled.gc

== transient noise

A transient noise source makes transtions to periodic random samples.

$ gnucap-mg-vams --cc trnoise.vams | g++ -xc++ `gnucap-conf --cppflags` -fPIC -shared - -o trnoise.so
$ gnucap trnoise.gc

== parameters and subdevices

An example involving parameters, local parameters, subdevices and $rdist.

$ gnucap-mg-vams --cc params.vams | g++ -xc++ `gnucap-conf --cppflags` -fPIC -shared - -o params.so
$ gnucap params.gc

see also tests/mg_rdist.tern.0.gc

== monte carlo

Simple example with a resistor subject to process parameters. Note that
repeated dc runs are inefficient, as they repeatedly recomute the bias point.
Modify d_dc.cc to sweeps the seed like a temperature.

$ gnucap-mg-vams --cc mc_res.vams | g++ -xc++ `gnucap-conf --cppflags` -fPIC -shared - -o mc_res.so
$ gnucap mc_res.gc

A sweep also facilitates postprocessing (here: with a parameter cheat).

$ gnucap mc_res_swp.gc | tail -n 3
imin=-0.00494296042597853
imax=-0.00481391535140574
irms= 0.00488693645873694
